WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.038
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Privileged Access.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:11.225
This is Chapter 3: Conditional Access for
administrators, and the activation gate.

4
00:00:11.225 --> 00:00:15.434
These policies are the program's first
writes to a tenant, so they're files a

5
00:00:15.434 --> 00:00:19.713
reviewer reads, and the code that writes
them does nothing until it's told to.

6
00:00:19.713 --> 00:00:23.819
We'll start from Microsoft's
own template for administrators,

7
00:00:23.819 --> 00:00:27.846
see what it leaves out, then walk
the files, the pre-merge check,

8
00:00:27.846 --> 00:00:30.987
the plan and the dry run,
two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:30.987 --> 00:00:36.490
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.340
Why does this chapter matter so much?

11
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.378
Because it's the first time
the program writes to a tenant.

12
00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:49.505
Book 04 gives it three things to
build: the administrator policy set,

13
00:00:49.505 --> 00:00:54.517
the gate that demands a fresh phishing-resistant
proof from a compliant device at

14
00:00:54.517 --> 00:00:59.528
the moment someone elevates, and the custom
authentication strength both rely on.

15
00:00:59.528 --> 00:01:03.302
Unlike the first two chapters,
this code can change things.

16
00:01:03.302 --> 00:01:08.940
So its design is cautious on purpose: it
writes only when told, creates every new

17
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:14.860
policy in report-only, enforces only with
a separate switch, and never deletes.

18
00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:22.273
Learn sets the order, and getting
it wrong leaves no gate at all.

19
00:01:22.273 --> 00:01:26.742
You create and switch on the policy
for the authentication context first,

20
00:01:26.742 --> 00:01:29.160
and only then select that context in PIM.

21
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.678
If no policy targets it, PIM falls back
to asking for multifactor sign-in,

22
00:01:33.678 --> 00:01:38.273
but that fallback doesn't happen when
the policy is off or only reporting.

23
00:01:38.273 --> 00:01:42.465
And if you also scope the policy to
a directory role, it never applies,

24
00:01:42.465 --> 00:01:46.059
because at activation the person
doesn't hold the role yet.

25
00:01:46.059 --> 00:01:50.490
So this chapter builds the gate, and
leaves selecting it to Chapter 4.

26
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.603
Next, which cloud.

27
00:01:55.603 --> 00:01:59.180
Every call in this chapter is
on the stable Graph version,

28
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.691
except protected actions, which is beta.

29
00:02:01.691 --> 00:02:06.271
For this estate's GCC tenant the host
is the worldwide Graph endpoint,

30
00:02:06.271 --> 00:02:11.415
and every method used lists the government
clouds in its availability table.

31
00:02:11.415 --> 00:02:15.702
Conditional Access and PIM are
available in the government clouds too.

32
00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:20.111
One thing does change there: the
two policies about Azure management

33
00:02:20.111 --> 00:02:24.752
name a different application, so a GCC
High program edits those two files.

34
00:02:24.752 --> 00:02:27.990
The code never assumes
a cloud; you name it.

35
00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.928
Microsoft's starting point
is its policy templates.

36
00:02:34.928 --> 00:02:40.069
The protect-administrators group includes
one requiring phishing-resistant sign-in

37
00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:44.782
for administrators, and its Learn page is
the procedure: at least fourteen roles,

38
00:02:44.782 --> 00:02:46.710
exclude the emergency accounts,

39
00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:50.852
use the built-in phishing-resistant
strength, and start in report-only.

40
00:02:50.852 --> 00:02:54.511
The same templates come back from
Graph as policy definitions,

41
00:02:54.511 --> 00:02:59.365
and that's the only administrator policy
Microsoft publishes on Learn in that form.

42
00:02:59.365 --> 00:03:03.939
So the repository pins the page
unmodified, checks its hashes, and converts

43
00:03:03.939 --> 00:03:08.730
the template without fixing anything,
then checks it like any other file.

44
00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.237
This is the heart of the chapter:

45
00:03:14.237 --> 00:03:18.155
what happens if you create Microsoft's
template exactly as published.

46
00:03:18.155 --> 00:03:21.068
The targeting is good, so
it's kept as the model.

47
00:03:21.068 --> 00:03:23.751
But the published example
lists only two roles

48
00:03:23.751 --> 00:03:26.229
where Learn's own
procedure wants fourteen.

49
00:03:26.229 --> 00:03:29.269
It grants plain multifactor
sign-in instead of the

50
00:03:29.269 --> 00:03:32.743
phishing-resistant strength,
and the two can't be combined.

51
00:03:32.743 --> 00:03:37.147
And it excludes a line of display text
where an object identifier belongs,

52
00:03:37.147 --> 00:03:40.830
which the admin center would
replace for you, but a script won't.

53
00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.310
Each of these is pinned by a test.

54
00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.485
The rest of the gaps
are the dangerous ones.

55
00:03:49.485 --> 00:03:52.850
The template doesn't exclude
the emergency access group,

56
00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:57.647
and those accounts must stay out of every
policy that blocks or restricts sign-in.

57
00:03:57.647 --> 00:04:01.634
It carries no state, so nothing
tells you to start in report-only.

58
00:04:01.634 --> 00:04:06.422
It has no authentication context, so
the activation gate isn't there at all.

59
00:04:06.422 --> 00:04:09.845
And it's just a read: nothing
compares it with the tenant,

60
00:04:09.845 --> 00:04:12.735
so a policy edited in
the portal drifts unseen.

61
00:04:12.735 --> 00:04:15.930
The verdict: keep the template's
targeting as the model,

62
00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.200
and never create it as published.

63
00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:22.888
Now the code.

64
00:04:22.888 --> 00:04:27.786
This chapter adds its own module,
its own call list, an entry script,

65
00:04:27.786 --> 00:04:32.775
and the policy files themselves, one
file per object, as Book 04 asks.

66
00:04:32.775 --> 00:04:36.717
There are the two authentication
contexts, the custom strength,

67
00:04:36.717 --> 00:04:40.362
the five administrator policies,
and the two gate policies,

68
00:04:40.362 --> 00:04:43.412
one for activation and
one for protected actions.

69
00:04:43.412 --> 00:04:48.562
The activation gate demands the strength
and a compliant device, every single time.

70
00:04:48.562 --> 00:04:53.403
Placeholders resolve from a per-tenant map,
except the strength, which is looked up

71
00:04:53.403 --> 00:04:58.250
by name in the tenant, so a policy can
never point at a strength that isn't there.

72
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.292
Before anything merges,
Test-PaCaRepository reads the files,

73
00:05:06.292 --> 00:05:09.575
and only the files, in
the pull request pipeline.

74
00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.325
The nine rules are on the slide.

75
00:05:11.325 --> 00:05:16.262
Most come from Learn: exclude the emergency
group, don't scope the gate to a role,

76
00:05:16.262 --> 00:05:20.658
don't mix plain multifactor with a
strength, cover the fourteen roles,

77
00:05:20.658 --> 00:05:23.975
and keep context identifiers
in the stricter range.

78
00:05:23.975 --> 00:05:26.064
A few are the program's own design,

79
00:05:26.064 --> 00:05:29.700
like refusing display text
where an identifier belongs.

80
00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:33.722
On the repository as merged, the
check passes with five warnings,

81
00:05:33.722 --> 00:05:38.630
and they're deliberate: the rollout
hasn't switched those policies on yet.

82
00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:46.192
One rule needs a closer look,
because the sources don't line up.

83
00:05:46.192 --> 00:05:49.321
Learn says report-only
policies don't block anyone,

84
00:05:49.321 --> 00:05:51.929
so they don't need the
emergency exclusion.

85
00:05:51.929 --> 00:05:57.364
Book 04's pre-merge check is stricter,
and fails any report-only file without it,

86
00:05:57.364 --> 00:06:00.505
even the probe that's
meant to exclude nobody.

87
00:06:00.505 --> 00:06:04.905
The chapter's finding is that the
stricter rule isn't wrong, just stricter.

88
00:06:04.905 --> 00:06:08.959
So the code puts the exclusion in
every file whatever its state,

89
00:06:08.959 --> 00:06:12.155
because enforcing a file
means changing one word.

90
00:06:12.155 --> 00:06:15.166
Only a named report-only
instrument may skip it,

91
00:06:15.166 --> 00:06:18.220
and it fails the moment
someone enables it.

92
00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:24.532
Here's how a change actually
reaches the tenant.

93
00:06:24.532 --> 00:06:28.307
The code reads the tenant's
policies, contexts and strengths,

94
00:06:28.307 --> 00:06:32.987
then compares each file with what's
there, property by property, but only the

95
00:06:32.987 --> 00:06:37.819
properties the file sets, so the noise
Graph returns doesn't look like a change.

96
00:06:37.819 --> 00:06:39.284
It plans in Learn's order,

97
00:06:39.284 --> 00:06:42.945
the contexts and strength before
the policies that use them.

98
00:06:42.945 --> 00:06:46.895
Every plan line gets an action,
and nothing is ever deleted.

99
00:06:46.895 --> 00:06:50.007
Without the apply switch, it
can't send a write at all.

100
00:06:50.007 --> 00:06:52.834
With it, it still refuses
if the check found a

101
00:06:52.834 --> 00:06:56.490
problem or a policy lacks
the emergency exclusion.

102
00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.173
The chapter keeps a short record of
where Learn's pages don't agree,

103
00:07:04.173 --> 00:07:06.940
so a later edit doesn't
quietly loosen the code.

104
00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.891
The biggest is the every-time
sign-in frequency:

105
00:07:09.891 --> 00:07:14.657
the Graph reference doesn't list it for
this use, but the PIM pages say to use it,

106
00:07:14.657 --> 00:07:18.440
so the file follows PIM and the
lab confirms Graph accepts it.

107
00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:22.165
Context identifiers follow
the stricter of two ranges.

108
00:07:22.165 --> 00:07:25.646
And protected actions, called
a preview in the outline,

109
00:07:25.646 --> 00:07:30.210
carry no preview label on Learn, but
their Graph assignment is beta only,

110
00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:33.590
so the code defines the
context and stops there.

111
00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:38.402
Who is this for?

112
00:07:38.402 --> 00:07:41.907
Developers build it, and
identity engineers and the I

113
00:07:41.907 --> 00:07:45.491
S S O's review every Conditional
Access change it makes.

114
00:07:45.491 --> 00:07:48.628
The permissions split
cleanly into two tasks.

115
00:07:48.628 --> 00:07:50.895
Reading needs three read permissions,

116
00:07:50.895 --> 00:07:54.578
and the code picks narrower ones
than the obvious broad choice.

117
00:07:54.578 --> 00:07:58.578
Applying adds exactly two write
permissions, and nothing else.

118
00:07:58.578 --> 00:08:02.287
Learn notes a known issue that may
ask for more consent on create,

119
00:08:02.287 --> 00:08:05.016
and the test-tenant lab
records whether it does.

120
00:08:05.016 --> 00:08:09.440
A test keeps each task's permissions
equal to the calls it makes.

121
00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:18.153
Lab 1 needs only a laptop:
PowerShell 7 and Pester, no tenant.

122
00:08:18.153 --> 00:08:22.725
You run the tests on Microsoft's template
as published, list the permissions for

123
00:08:22.725 --> 00:08:27.366
reading and for applying, and run the
pre-merge check on the repository's own files.

124
00:08:27.366 --> 00:08:32.415
It passes with the five expected warnings;
a real finding would fail the pipeline.

125
00:08:32.415 --> 00:08:36.503
Then the revealing step: run the
same check on Microsoft's template.

126
00:08:36.503 --> 00:08:41.633
It comes back with two findings and two
warnings, the missing emergency exclusion,

127
00:08:41.633 --> 00:08:44.500
the display text, the
plain multifactor grant,

128
00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.420
and twelve of the fourteen roles missing.

129
00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:54.895
Next you plan against a fictional
tenant without sending anything.

130
00:08:54.895 --> 00:08:58.892
The plan explains itself: the
context and strength get created,

131
00:08:58.892 --> 00:09:03.581
two objects get small updates, one
matches, and three policies are blocked,

132
00:09:03.581 --> 00:09:07.270
because the strength and context
they need don't exist yet.

133
00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:10.420
A portal-made policy is
reported, never deleted.

134
00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:12.995
Then you apply to the fake three times.

135
00:09:12.995 --> 00:09:17.342
The first run creates the building
blocks, the second creates the three

136
00:09:17.342 --> 00:09:21.320
blocked policies in report-only,
and the third finds nothing to do.

137
00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:24.470
That's what a safe,
repeatable write looks like.

138
00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:33.095
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant, never
production, and it's careful about order.

139
00:09:33.095 --> 00:09:35.553
Build Chapter 2's
emergency accounts first,

140
00:09:35.553 --> 00:09:39.649
and register phishing-resistant
methods for your test administrators

141
00:09:39.649 --> 00:09:43.882
before anything is enforced, because
Learn warns you can lock yourself out.

142
00:09:43.882 --> 00:09:47.983
Then write your map, register a
read-only app, and read the plan.

143
00:09:47.983 --> 00:09:52.882
Add the write permissions and apply:
every new policy lands in report-only.

144
00:09:52.882 --> 00:09:55.032
Watch the results for at least a week.

145
00:09:55.032 --> 00:09:58.571
Then enforce the gate on its
own, in a separate change.

146
00:09:58.571 --> 00:10:02.170
Backing out means turning
policies off, never deleting.

147
00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.549
Every danger here has a guard,

148
00:10:07.549 --> 00:10:11.620
and the theme is that nothing important
depends on someone remembering.

149
00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:15.723
The worst is enforcing a policy
before the emergency accounts work,

150
00:10:15.723 --> 00:10:19.532
which is the same lockout as Chapter
2, just by a different route.

151
00:10:19.532 --> 00:10:24.525
The quietest is selecting the gate in PIM
while its policy is still report-only:

152
00:10:24.525 --> 00:10:26.907
you get no gate and no fallback either.

153
00:10:26.907 --> 00:10:31.856
Others are about speed and reach: switching
policies straight on, a pipeline that

154
00:10:31.856 --> 00:10:36.870
deletes, and a write permission left
lying on the app after the change is done.

155
00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:41.970
Here's what done looks like.

156
00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:46.353
On the file side: the check runs on
every pull request with no findings,

157
00:10:46.353 --> 00:10:51.583
every file carries the emergency exclusion,
the administrator policy covers all

158
00:10:51.583 --> 00:10:56.658
fourteen roles, and the activation gate
is switched on before anyone selects it.

159
00:10:56.658 --> 00:11:00.181
On the run side: every plan
was read as a dry run first,

160
00:11:00.181 --> 00:11:04.628
every new policy landed in
report-only, each enforcement was its

161
00:11:04.628 --> 00:11:08.907
own reviewed change, and only
the pipeline holds write access.

162
00:11:08.907 --> 00:11:14.732
Next, Chapter 4 builds PIM for Microsoft
Entra roles as code, and selects this gate.

163
00:11:14.732 --> 00:11:15.900
Thanks for watching.
